        "name": "Bash",
        "description": "Executes a shell command and returns its output. This is the way to inspect the machine and to run programs: list files, read what a service returns, check processes, ports, and environment variables.\n\n- The shell starts in the directory the previous call left it in, so `cd` persists between calls. Exported variables and other shell state do not; pass a sequence to one command or use absolute paths.\n- The shell is non-interactive. Anything that waits for input blocks until the timeout.\n- `timeout` is in seconds (default 60, max 600). A foreground command still running at its timeout is not killed: it moves to the background and the call answers with a job handle instead of an error.\n- `run_in_background` returns at once with `job_id`, `pid` and an `output_file` that is written while the command runs. Use it for a server or a long job: start it once, read the output with JobOutput and stop it with JobKill. Do not start a second copy to check on it, and do not use `ps`/`pkill`, which many images do not have. A background command runs until it exits; it stops only at its own `timeout` (when you set one) or through JobKill.",
        "input_schema": {
